WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.380
Everything in the first film was a drawing: a kit, a box and a water tank.

2
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.840
How do we know a real plant cell has a box?

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.260
And how big is one?

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.990
A real celery cell is too small to see with your eyes alone.

5
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.330
To check the model's parts, you need a light microscope and a way to measure.

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.013
First, make a wet mount.

7
00:00:28.293 --> 00:00:33.873
A thin piece of onion skin sits in a drop of iodine stain, under a thin glass cover

8
00:00:33.873 --> 00:00:34.873
slip.

9
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:37.407
Light passes up through the thin layer.

10
00:00:38.687 --> 00:00:41.327
Through the eyepiece, you see a round circle of light.

11
00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:46.987
Inside it, onion skin cells sit side by side, like long boxes.

12
00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:50.627
The stain makes each nucleus look darker.

13
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.260
Notice what is missing.

14
00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.220
These onion cells are not green.

15
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.100
They have walls, but no developed chloroplasts.

16
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:04.343
Now a waterweed leaf, mounted in a drop of water.

17
00:01:05.603 --> 00:01:09.483
Its cells are boxy too, and most are packed with green chloroplasts.

18
00:01:10.483 --> 00:01:14.883
Last, a few cells from the inside of a cheek, colored with a blue stain.

19
00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:21.003
They have no box. They are thin and flat, like tiles on a floor.

20
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:23.477
So the box is real.

21
00:01:24.737 --> 00:01:26.257
The plant cells show straight walls.

22
00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:30.677
The cheek cells show only a thin outline, with no wall.

23
00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.390
Now, how big? Scientists measure cells in micrometres.

24
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.170
The screen shows how small one micrometre is.

25
00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:45.243
Scientists use a tiny ruler inside the eyepiece.

26
00:01:46.423 --> 00:01:48.943
In class, you can use the circle of light itself.

27
00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:53.223
Count similar cells lying end to end across it.

28
00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:56.583
Then divide the circle's width by that count.

29
00:01:57.723 --> 00:02:00.083
That gives a rough average width for one cell.

30
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.727
Try a made-up class example with cheek cells.

31
00:02:04.947 --> 00:02:07.727
The circle's width and the cell count are on the screen.

32
00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:10.367
About how wide is one cell?

33
00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.030
Divide, and the answer is on the screen.

34
00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.110
One published study measured cheek cells at nearly the same width.

35
00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.550
A rough count lands close.

36
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:29.913
One more surprise.

37
00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:36.293
Scientists found small clusters of colorless cells inside green waterweed leaves.

38
00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:39.353
These cells had no chloroplasts.

39
00:02:40.317 --> 00:02:43.837
A dye still lit up a large central vacuole inside them.

40
00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:48.457
So even in one green leaf, not every plant cell is green.

41
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.720
Look and measure. The box is real, and so are the green chloroplasts and the water

42
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.460
tank. But only some plant cells are green, and cells come in many shapes and sizes.
